WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:29.237
- So one of the reasons that I was
really excited to do this is

00:00:29.237 --> 00:00:31.823
cuz obviously I'm combining
two of my favorite things:

00:00:31.823 --> 00:00:32.991
sports and movies.

00:00:32.991 --> 00:00:33.825
- OK.

00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:36.745
- So we have to start there
with your top five sports movies,

00:00:36.745 --> 00:00:38.997
and they have to be in order.

00:00:38.997 --> 00:00:39.998
- Oh, that's tough. In order?

00:00:39.998 --> 00:00:40.832
- Yes.
- Um—

00:00:40.832 --> 00:00:42.584
- Cuz, you know, the copout
is just to say them all.

00:00:42.584 --> 00:00:43.543
You gotta rank the sports movies.

00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:46.129
- I gotta rank the sports movies. OK.

00:00:46.129 --> 00:00:53.762
I'm going with...No. 1...

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:58.516
oh, man. "White Men Can't Jump."

00:00:58.516 --> 00:01:00.101
- Great, great start.

00:01:00.101 --> 00:01:02.062
- No. 2, I'mma go with

00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:03.396
"Any Given Sunday."

00:01:05.356 --> 00:01:07.525
3, I'm going with "Sandlot."

00:01:07.525 --> 00:01:09.152
- Mm.

00:01:09.152 --> 00:01:13.740
- 4, I'm gonna go with...

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:20.622
"Remember the Titans." And 5,
I'mma go with "Hardball." I'm biased.

00:01:20.622 --> 00:01:22.624
- Yeah, gotta pick "Hardball."
- Yeah, yeah.

00:01:22.624 --> 00:01:25.210
- There is a big one missing,
but this is all opinion.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:26.628
There was no, "He Got Game."

00:01:26.628 --> 00:01:28.088
- Oh, man, yes.

00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:30.632
- Yeah, see, I'mma save you there.
There was no "He Got Game."

00:01:30.632 --> 00:01:33.426
- "He Got Game." Yes, all right. I'll swap,

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:35.386
I'll swap "Remember the
Titans" and "He Got Game."

00:01:35.386 --> 00:01:37.305
- OK.
- Yeah.
- All right.

00:01:37.305 --> 00:01:39.057
Are we missing any other big ones?

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:43.978
I mean, are you like a, are you
like a "Major League"?

00:01:43.978 --> 00:01:45.188
Are you a "Jerry Maguire"?

00:01:45.188 --> 00:01:46.147
Are you any of—?

00:01:46.147 --> 00:01:49.067
- Ah, they don't really rank.

00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:51.152
I was gonna say "Space Jam 1."
- Yeah.

00:01:51.152 --> 00:01:54.405
- You know what I'm saying?
Just as a straight classic,

00:01:54.405 --> 00:01:57.659
but I don't know. "Field of Dreams" was,

00:01:57.659 --> 00:01:59.119
I guess, does that count? That counts, right?

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:00.245
- Yeah, that counts.
- Yeah.

00:02:00.245 --> 00:02:01.955
- Such an unrealistic plot—

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:04.499
- "Mighty Ducks."
- "Mighty Ducks."

00:02:04.499 --> 00:02:07.502
- Like, that's a good one.
- There's too many.
- Yes.
- But your list is solid.

00:02:07.502 --> 00:02:09.712
So I'll take that.
- OK.
- And we can start there.

00:02:09.712 --> 00:02:11.172
Yeah, you passed that test.

00:02:11.172 --> 00:02:13.091
You started with a really
good one, "White Men Can't Jump."

00:02:13.091 --> 00:02:15.301
You have to, you have to
begin with that, and you put

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:17.762
"He Got Game" in there. So
it all worked out.

00:02:17.762 --> 00:02:18.096
- OK.

00:02:18.096 --> 00:02:19.764
- But you of course were
in "Friday Night Lights."

00:02:19.764 --> 00:02:22.058
You did a sports TV show.
- Mmhmm.

00:02:22.058 --> 00:02:24.561
- What do you like doing
better, TV shows or movies?

00:02:24.561 --> 00:02:26.771
- I think—that's a good question.

00:02:26.771 --> 00:02:30.817
I think it evolved over time.

00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:37.490
I used to enjoy doing
television because it gave,

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:41.202
it gave the actor time to
develop and grow the character.

00:02:41.202 --> 00:02:44.497
It gave time for the audience
to actually fall in love with

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:46.291
the character.

00:02:47.292 --> 00:02:49.210
Look, I don't know how much, like, sympathy

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:51.462
and empathy you would have
for, like, Wallace on "The Wire"

00:02:51.462 --> 00:02:53.423
if it was only like one episode, you know?

00:02:53.423 --> 00:02:54.799
Rather than, you know,

00:02:54.799 --> 00:02:58.344
an entire season to be able to
kinda like, really, like, make

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:02.849
his death that much more tragic
and meaningful, you know?
- Yeah.

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:05.268
- And then I think there was a
time where I didn't know if I

00:03:05.268 --> 00:03:08.438
could open a film or
carry a movie, you know?

00:03:08.438 --> 00:03:09.564
Was I a leading man?

00:03:09.564 --> 00:03:12.358
So, like, "Fruitvale" kinda
answered a lot of

00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:15.695
those questions for me at
a young, at a, you know, younger age.

00:03:17.322 --> 00:03:20.200
And I enjoyed, you know, a
beginning and an end, you know?

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.995
I kinda know where I'm going
so I can arc a character out.

00:03:23.995 --> 00:03:26.998
I could actually, like, be
intentional with my choices,

00:03:26.998 --> 00:03:28.666
knowing where I'm gonna go. In TV,

00:03:28.666 --> 00:03:30.793
you don't really know
where the next season's

00:03:30.793 --> 00:03:31.502
gonna take you.

00:03:31.502 --> 00:03:32.462
You don't know what the, you know,

00:03:32.462 --> 00:03:34.339
what's really gonna happen
with your character.

00:03:34.339 --> 00:03:35.924
So you're just, you're in the moment.

00:03:35.924 --> 00:03:39.093
So I think it's just a
different muscle and a want.

00:03:39.093 --> 00:03:41.763
And I think now at this
age, you know,

00:03:41.763 --> 00:03:43.681
you're looking for, you know,

00:03:43.681 --> 00:03:45.308
the best material.
- Mmhmm.

00:03:45.308 --> 00:03:48.311
If it's a character that's
really rich and interesting,

00:03:48.311 --> 00:03:49.520
I might want more time.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:53.441
- I think that movies leave
you feeling satisfied.
- Mmhmm.

00:03:53.441 --> 00:03:56.903
- But there is something very
intimate about television shows

00:03:56.903 --> 00:04:00.365
because you're almost inviting
those people into your home,

00:04:00.365 --> 00:04:03.159
right? Like, every single week on Wednesday

00:04:03.159 --> 00:04:05.578
or whatever the day is, I'm
hanging out with these people.

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:08.122
And I feel like I know them.
- Exactly.
- And there's just,

00:04:08.122 --> 00:04:10.041
there is more of a connection there,

00:04:10.041 --> 00:04:13.253
but I don't know if you
always feel as full.

00:04:13.253 --> 00:04:15.880
So there's something nice about
having this full character

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:17.840
that's in a movie.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:18.258
- Yeah.

00:04:18.258 --> 00:04:20.843
- You said something
interesting talking about

00:04:20.843 --> 00:04:22.637
when you were doing "Fruitvale Station."
- Mmhmm.

00:04:22.637 --> 00:04:26.099
- You said that you would ask
yourself, "Am I a leading man?"
- Mmhmm.

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:29.143
- When did you get that definitive
answer that you were a

00:04:29.143 --> 00:04:30.186
leading man?

00:04:30.186 --> 00:04:33.439
And how long did you
struggle with that question?

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:34.774
- That's a good question.

00:04:36.985 --> 00:04:37.819
I think...

00:04:41.406 --> 00:04:47.495
I think that, on paper, answered
the question with, you know,

00:04:47.495 --> 00:04:51.582
the success that movie
had. I think probably

00:04:53.584 --> 00:05:02.802
"Creed 1," for me, really
kinda, it kinda resonated a bit more.

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:05.054
I think there's a lot of, like,
an imposter syndrome that

00:05:05.054 --> 00:05:10.351
followed during that time
of figuring out, you know,

00:05:10.351 --> 00:05:13.813
your place in the industry,
your place in, you know,

00:05:13.813 --> 00:05:16.482
the movie business where
you're transitioning from TV to

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:17.400
film, you know,

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.653
at a time where there was
a shift in, like, what was

00:05:20.653 --> 00:05:26.159
acceptable, you know? And
yeah, you just, you know,

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:28.202
having faith in what you're doing.

00:05:28.202 --> 00:05:31.080
But I think after "Creed 1,"
it started to really, like,

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.166
set in. And then, like, you know,

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:37.962
your family and your close
friends, they, you know,

00:05:37.962 --> 00:05:41.215
having to remind you of where you are.
- Yeah.

00:05:41.215 --> 00:05:43.718
I used to like try to, you know,

00:05:43.718 --> 00:05:45.428
I don't believe my own hype.

00:05:45.428 --> 00:05:47.430
I don't drink my own
Kool-Aid a lil' bit,

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:50.475
I just, you know, I like
doing the work and, you know,

00:05:50.475 --> 00:05:53.561
and trying to be the
best version of myself.

00:05:53.561 --> 00:05:57.065
- So there's this quote,
it's by this Greek writer

00:05:57.065 --> 00:05:58.608
named Nikos.
- Mmhmm.

00:05:58.608 --> 00:06:02.111
- And he said, "I once saw
a bee drown in honey,

00:06:02.111 --> 00:06:03.279
and I understood."

00:06:04.238 --> 00:06:07.450
And that's kind of what I think
about when I hear you say—
- That's a good one.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:10.453
- You know, "I was just trying
to make sure I stayed level,

00:06:10.453 --> 00:06:11.871
and there was all this,
like, goodness around me."
- Mmhmm.

00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:13.956
- "And there's so many people telling
me about all this goodness

00:06:13.956 --> 00:06:19.629
around me, but I don't want
to be overpowered by that."

00:06:19.629 --> 00:06:22.882
But I'm sure that also, though,
is a struggle in itself too.

00:06:22.882 --> 00:06:25.551
- It's a balance between,
like, owning your blessings

00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:28.012
and your success in the
path that you've been on

00:06:28.012 --> 00:06:31.432
and the things that you went
through to get what you have,

00:06:31.432 --> 00:06:35.645
you know? And remembering
where they came from.

00:06:35.645 --> 00:06:38.439
I think when you're living
in your purpose and you're

00:06:38.439 --> 00:06:41.651
blessed enough to feel like
what you're doing every day is

00:06:41.651 --> 00:06:44.195
something that you're
supposed to be doing,

00:06:44.195 --> 00:06:45.696
you know, that's a blessing,
and you gotta live in that,

00:06:45.696 --> 00:06:49.659
you know? All of the other
things around it start to make it

00:06:49.659 --> 00:06:50.993
complicated.

00:06:51.953 --> 00:06:54.080
But I think the success
and stuff just magnifies who

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:57.375
you really are.
- Yeah.
- You know?

00:06:57.375 --> 00:06:59.127
Yeah, but this Hollywood
thing's a monster. You see,

00:06:59.127 --> 00:07:02.839
you know, people, you
know—it's undefeated.

00:07:02.839 --> 00:07:06.843
- Yeah. Yeah, it's a–I think
it's easy to get caught up

00:07:06.843 --> 00:07:09.262
in things you probably
shouldn't be caught up in.

00:07:10.138 --> 00:07:12.932
But one of the great things
about you, and I mean this,

00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:16.144
I have never heard anyone say
anything other than the fact

00:07:16.144 --> 00:07:18.104
that you are the nicest human being.

00:07:18.104 --> 00:07:19.105
- I'll take that.
- Yeah.

00:07:19.105 --> 00:07:22.608
Like, everyone just talks about
your smile and how kind you

00:07:22.608 --> 00:07:24.902
are and that you're just genuine.

00:07:24.902 --> 00:07:27.363
And I think that that shows
with things like what you're

00:07:27.363 --> 00:07:30.241
working on now, like the
Invesco QQQ Legacy Classic,

00:07:30.241 --> 00:07:33.453
which is gonna highlight HBCUs.

00:07:33.453 --> 00:07:35.413
When I think about that title, though,

00:07:35.413 --> 00:07:38.249
obviously what sticks out to
me is the legacy part of it.

00:07:38.249 --> 00:07:39.417
And I want to talk about HBCUs,

00:07:39.417 --> 00:07:41.794
but I want to talk a little
bit about you too first.

00:07:41.794 --> 00:07:44.547
I know you've done a lot
of HBCU talking already.
- Mmhmm.

00:07:44.547 --> 00:07:48.468
But how often do you think about legacy?

00:07:48.468 --> 00:07:49.677
- All the time.

00:07:49.677 --> 00:07:51.262
I think about legacy a lot.

00:07:51.262 --> 00:07:51.846
You know,

00:07:51.846 --> 00:07:53.890
when you think about things
you want to be a part of,

00:07:54.807 --> 00:07:56.267
not just a flash in the pan,

00:07:56.267 --> 00:07:58.352
something that's gonna stand, you know,

00:07:58.352 --> 00:07:59.562
withstand the test of time,

00:07:59.562 --> 00:08:02.356
that's going to be around
longer than, you know,

00:08:02.356 --> 00:08:05.818
than my physical body is.
- Mmhmm.
- You know, life's short.

00:08:05.818 --> 00:08:09.071
So you think about how
you want to be remembered.

00:08:10.323 --> 00:08:12.617
Who did I help? Who did I put on?

00:08:12.617 --> 00:08:18.080
Who did I, you know, nudge
in the right direction

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:19.540
so they can achieve their dreams,

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:23.461
you know?
- Mmhmm.
- There's, that
is, like, things that,

00:08:23.461 --> 00:08:27.590
those are the things that I
think about, you know, a lot.

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:29.133
And so, you know, with,

00:08:29.133 --> 00:08:34.138
you know, with Invesco QQQ
and the Legacy Classic,

00:08:34.138 --> 00:08:39.560
it was an opportunity for me
to really try to pay it forward

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:43.064
and open up the platform and
the space for these, you know,

00:08:43.064 --> 00:08:46.108
HBCUs that's been overlooked
for such a long time,

00:08:46.108 --> 00:08:47.902
especially in the sports, you know...

00:08:49.862 --> 00:08:52.823
You know, field losing, you
know, top prospects, you know,

00:08:52.823 --> 00:08:56.118
year after year to, like,
these bigger D-I schools that,

00:08:56.118 --> 00:08:58.162
you know, obviously, you know, the goals,

00:08:58.162 --> 00:08:59.455
you know, for a lot of these athletes is,

00:08:59.455 --> 00:09:01.249
you know, get to the league.

00:09:01.249 --> 00:09:06.087
So to be able to make HBCUs
or help put them in a position

00:09:06.087 --> 00:09:10.883
to be destination points,
- Mmhmm.
- I think was part of the way

00:09:10.883 --> 00:09:13.386
of thinking
- Yeah.
- when it came to putting together this one.

00:09:13.386 --> 00:09:14.554
- Yeah.

00:09:14.554 --> 00:09:17.306
I actually remember seeing
an interview where you said

00:09:17.306 --> 00:09:21.018
that your greatest fear
was being forgotten.
- Mmhmm.

00:09:21.018 --> 00:09:22.186
- Is that still true?

00:09:22.186 --> 00:09:24.272
- Yes.
- OK, elaborate.

00:09:24.272 --> 00:09:27.149
Came from a personal place, I guess,

00:09:27.149 --> 00:09:28.109
of like...

00:09:29.819 --> 00:09:34.407
it was one of my partner's
birthday who passed away when I

00:09:34.407 --> 00:09:38.035
was younger. And it was the day
after his birthday, and I had

00:09:38.035 --> 00:09:42.248
forgot that it was his
birthday.
- Mmm.
- And that feeling,

00:09:42.248 --> 00:09:45.209
I was like, oh, man, OK.

00:09:45.209 --> 00:09:46.961
Yeah, life's crazy right now,

00:09:46.961 --> 00:09:49.046
ripping and running all over the place.

00:09:49.046 --> 00:09:51.549
And there was a moment of like, wow, if I,

00:09:51.549 --> 00:09:54.010
I had a moment where I actually, I forgot

00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:55.553
that that was his birthday
and I'm, first time that's

00:09:55.553 --> 00:09:57.013
ever happened.

00:09:57.013 --> 00:10:00.683
That feeling of and that
realization of, you know,

00:10:00.683 --> 00:10:03.769
mortality, and people move on
and life goes on, you know,

00:10:03.769 --> 00:10:08.441
and people might forget
about you in moments of time.

00:10:08.441 --> 00:10:09.525
Doesn't mean that you're not important.

00:10:09.525 --> 00:10:11.360
Doesn't mean that the love or that bond

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:12.862
or that relationship wasn't there.

00:10:12.862 --> 00:10:15.781
It's just,
- Mmhmm.
- There's the reality of that.

00:10:15.781 --> 00:10:21.579
So that fear is something
that I think in moments...

00:10:21.579 --> 00:10:25.041
pushes you to, you know,
to make your life worth it,

00:10:25.041 --> 00:10:28.002
you know, to make every moment count.

00:10:28.002 --> 00:10:31.130
To leave something behind
that you could be proud of,

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:34.133
that other people could
look to and be, you know, inspired by.

00:10:36.344 --> 00:10:38.929
Yeah, I think that
those are, yeah, so that.

00:10:38.929 --> 00:10:39.889
- Yeah.

00:10:39.889 --> 00:10:40.723
- It's a good, yeah, yeah, forget,

00:10:40.723 --> 00:10:41.432
yeah.

00:10:41.432 --> 00:10:42.058
- Yeah.

00:10:42.058 --> 00:10:43.017
- Good one.

00:10:43.017 --> 00:10:44.268
- Claire Underwood,

00:10:44.268 --> 00:10:47.563
great television character
- Mmhmm.
- played by Robin Wright Penn.

00:10:47.563 --> 00:10:51.108
In an episode, she said,
"I don't want to be famous.

00:10:51.108 --> 00:10:53.027
I want to be significant."
- Mmhmm.

00:10:53.027 --> 00:10:53.527
- And I was like,

00:10:53.527 --> 00:10:56.155
there actually is, like, this
small difference to that.

00:10:56.155 --> 00:11:00.660
And I think that what you're
describing is significance.

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:02.620
That you want, like, you
being here to mean something,

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:03.954
like, much bigger than the fact that I was

00:11:03.954 --> 00:11:05.790
this famous movie star.
- Yeah.

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:08.334
- But, like, there was this,
this change to that,

00:11:08.334 --> 00:11:09.168
right?

00:11:09.168 --> 00:11:11.671
And there's something that
is going to last forever

00:11:11.671 --> 00:11:13.255
and leave a bigger impact.

00:11:13.255 --> 00:11:15.591
And I think that does
start with things like

00:11:15.591 --> 00:11:17.968
this Legacy Classic with Invesco QQQ.

00:11:17.968 --> 00:11:21.347
So I love that. HBCU specifically,

00:11:21.347 --> 00:11:23.724
you touched on why they are important.

00:11:23.724 --> 00:11:26.602
Do you feel a cultural
shift happening with HBCUs?

00:11:26.602 --> 00:11:27.436
- Definitely.

00:11:27.436 --> 00:11:27.770
- Yeah.

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:32.066
- You know, I think it's
the right climate. You know,

00:11:32.066 --> 00:11:37.655
all the stars are just, you
know, aligning in the right way

00:11:37.655 --> 00:11:40.616
where it's having a real moment.
- Mmhmm.

00:11:40.616 --> 00:11:43.327
- And you can't let it
pass you by, you know.

00:11:43.327 --> 00:11:45.996
You have to, like, harness
this energy and momentum.
- Yeah.

00:11:45.996 --> 00:11:48.499
-  And keep it moving in the right direction.

00:11:48.499 --> 00:11:52.169
And I feel like knowing
where you came from, knowing

00:11:52.169 --> 00:11:55.631
what's available to you,
what's in support of you.

00:11:56.924 --> 00:11:59.885
I think we naturally started
to gravitate towards that.

00:11:59.885 --> 00:12:03.597
And, you know, the importance
of the next generation

00:12:03.597 --> 00:12:05.599
of how they're taught,
what they understand,

00:12:05.599 --> 00:12:07.601
what they know about themselves.

00:12:07.601 --> 00:12:10.271
That sense of pride is going to be very,

00:12:10.271 --> 00:12:13.190
very crucially important to our future leaders,
- Yeah.
- you know?

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:20.614
And to see these young people
out here so strong, outspoken,

00:12:20.614 --> 00:12:22.616
you know, in the thick of it.

00:12:23.576 --> 00:12:28.664
It was very inspiring, and
it allowed me to, I don't know.

00:12:28.664 --> 00:12:30.666
It was a moment in time that, you know...

00:12:30.666 --> 00:12:32.877
You hear stories of like, you know,

00:12:32.877 --> 00:12:34.879
your grandmother and your mom
and all these other things

00:12:34.879 --> 00:12:36.714
having their moment, you
know what I'm saying?

00:12:36.714 --> 00:12:37.256
- Yeah.

00:12:37.256 --> 00:12:38.299
- And it seems...

00:12:38.299 --> 00:12:41.218
- Oh, the old stories, yeah, I love them.
- Seems so old, you know?

00:12:41.218 --> 00:12:43.220
And when it comes around
and was looking at you right

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:45.765
in the face, like, oh, this is our time.
- Yeah.

00:12:45.765 --> 00:12:47.349
- OK, I get that.

00:12:47.349 --> 00:12:50.853
You know, and that's a real life, you know, moment.

00:12:50.853 --> 00:12:52.688
So,
-Yeah.
- It was cool.

00:12:52.688 --> 00:12:55.941
- So I want to read this
quote and these stats

00:12:55.941 --> 00:12:59.737
that I saw about HBCUs.
- Mmhmm.
- Because, yes, they are so important

00:12:59.737 --> 00:13:03.449
to us, but HBCUs really
are vital to America.

00:13:03.449 --> 00:13:07.536
So Black colleges account
for just 3 percent of the four-year

00:13:07.536 --> 00:13:09.872
nonprofit colleges. Their alumni, though,

00:13:09.872 --> 00:13:12.833
account for roughly 80 percent of Black judges,

00:13:12.833 --> 00:13:15.169
50 percent of Black lawyers and doctors.

00:13:15.169 --> 00:13:18.047
Their students account for
25 percent of Black undergrads who

00:13:18.047 --> 00:13:20.007
earned degrees in STEM.

00:13:20.007 --> 00:13:23.761
So that's important for myriad reasons.
- Yes.

00:13:23.761 --> 00:13:25.888
- But you need Black doctors, right?
- A thousand percent.

00:13:25.888 --> 00:13:29.558
- Because you need people
that understand us, right?
- One thousand percent.

00:13:29.558 --> 00:13:33.020
- And the more that you have
Black people in these positions,

00:13:33.020 --> 00:13:34.980
the more you can close
certain disparities.
- Mmhmm.

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:38.359
- So HBCUs really are like
a motor that is keeping us

00:13:38.359 --> 00:13:39.985
running in a lot of spaces.

00:13:39.985 --> 00:13:42.196
And that is so important, but I do think

00:13:42.196 --> 00:13:45.741
the most important thing
about HBCUs existing

00:13:45.741 --> 00:13:48.536
is that you're never
the only Black person.
- Mmhmm.

00:13:48.536 --> 00:13:50.871
- You are always around
people that just, like,

00:13:50.871 --> 00:13:53.040
innately understand you,
- Mmhmm.
- right?

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:54.792
They get the same jokes, right?

00:13:54.792 --> 00:13:58.963
They feel the same way when
we are scrolling on our phones

00:13:58.963 --> 00:14:01.799
and seeing Black people dying every day.

00:14:01.799 --> 00:14:05.386
And it's important to have that
sense of community because,

00:14:05.386 --> 00:14:08.848
so I went to a PWI. That's
predominantly white institution,

00:14:08.848 --> 00:14:11.934
if nobody knows. Loved my
school, had a great time.

00:14:11.934 --> 00:14:15.396
But there were so many times
I was the only Black person

00:14:15.396 --> 00:14:16.689
in a classroom.

00:14:16.689 --> 00:14:19.275
- And I'm sure it has happened to you too.
- Mmhmm.

00:14:19.275 --> 00:14:21.318
being the only Black person in many rooms.
- Mmhmm.

00:14:23.904 --> 00:14:26.991
- Is there a time that you
really remember like, wow,

00:14:26.991 --> 00:14:30.286
I'm the only Black person in this room?

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:33.706
- Hmm. I mean, I think [exhaling]—

00:14:33.706 --> 00:14:37.167
I think the sad part of the
answer is that, you know,

00:14:37.167 --> 00:14:38.627
it's been that way for such a long time,

00:14:38.627 --> 00:14:39.670
you can't remember the first?

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:40.504
- 100 percent.

00:14:40.504 --> 00:14:43.716
- You know? But there, it's a...

00:14:43.716 --> 00:14:48.804
Like, I think we, we naturally
have become chameleons,

00:14:48.804 --> 00:14:52.141
you know, to adapt to our
environment, to, you know,

00:14:52.141 --> 00:14:53.934
from code-switching to whatever, right?
- Mmhmm.

00:14:53.934 --> 00:14:56.604
- Like that's—those are the
tools that at a young age,

00:14:56.604 --> 00:15:00.107
you learn to kind of
navigate at a certain,

00:15:00.107 --> 00:15:01.984
at a certain point.

00:15:01.984 --> 00:15:05.613
I think I've always had
the mindset, you know,

00:15:05.613 --> 00:15:07.031
through my parents, you know,

00:15:07.031 --> 00:15:13.746
of just being aware and never letting that

00:15:13.746 --> 00:15:17.499
influence me to feel like a
victim or whatever it may be.
- Right.

00:15:17.499 --> 00:15:21.128
- But just a sense of empowerment to use

00:15:21.128 --> 00:15:23.172
that perception to my advantage.

00:15:23.172 --> 00:15:24.006
- Yes.

00:15:24.006 --> 00:15:28.302
- So it's always been a
calculating thing for me to

00:15:28.302 --> 00:15:31.805
take those environments and
use them to my advantage,

00:15:31.805 --> 00:15:35.935
to, you know, whether it's
Trojan horse situations,

00:15:35.935 --> 00:15:36.810
you know what I'm sayin'?

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:41.398
That's, you know, where you
just, you know, quietly move in.

00:15:41.398 --> 00:15:44.777
And next thing you know, it's,
you know, we all in here. [Laughing]

00:15:44.777 --> 00:15:48.072
- Totally. And I mean,
and there has to be the first,

00:15:48.072 --> 00:15:48.697
like right?
- Yeah. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:15:48.697 --> 00:15:51.325
- There has to be the person
that gets this there

00:15:51.325 --> 00:15:53.953
and can create more space for more people.

00:15:53.953 --> 00:15:56.705
- I know 2020, of course,
is a watershed moment

00:15:56.705 --> 00:15:58.832
for everyone.

00:15:58.832 --> 00:16:02.169
You were super active
in all the protesting.

00:16:02.169 --> 00:16:06.006
Tell me three ways 2020 changed you.

00:16:06.674 --> 00:16:10.761
- Three ways 2020 changed me. Um...

00:16:10.761 --> 00:16:15.391
I think it, and this is, you
know, probably a cliche one.

00:16:15.391 --> 00:16:22.231
I think it, it made me realize
the importance of time and family.

00:16:22.231 --> 00:16:26.235
- Mmhmm.
- Being able to really spend time, you know,

00:16:26.235 --> 00:16:28.737
I'm an uncle—I'm an uncle now, so whatever.

00:16:28.737 --> 00:16:30.739
- [Laughing] Uncle Mike!

00:16:30.739 --> 00:16:31.824
- Uncle Mike.

00:16:32.825 --> 00:16:34.284
- They say there's nothing like that,

00:16:34.284 --> 00:16:36.745
like, niece, nephew love.
Like, it's just different.

00:16:36.745 --> 00:16:37.997
- It's different.
- Yup.

00:16:37.997 --> 00:16:42.918
- And I've never had to, I never
experienced that before, so, you know.

00:16:42.918 --> 00:16:45.087
- I love that.
- Being able to change diapers

00:16:45.087 --> 00:16:46.296
and be around and be present.

00:16:46.296 --> 00:16:47.047
- Are you good?

00:16:47.047 --> 00:16:47.881
- Yeah, I'm good.
- OK.

00:16:47.881 --> 00:16:51.218
- I've actually got great experiences
with changing diapers.

00:16:51.218 --> 00:16:54.054
Like, my sister's actually a
little pissed that, you know,

00:16:54.054 --> 00:16:57.016
I've gotten like the light
diapers all the time.

00:16:57.016 --> 00:16:59.518
- Yup.
- Literally, I've missed every blowout that's happened.

00:16:59.518 --> 00:17:02.396
- Not a lot of poop in the diaper yet. [Laughing]

00:17:02.396 --> 00:17:04.690
- "What?! Oh, you care about—you're
my guy. What's up, my guy? It's cool."

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:06.442
- Yeah, he's like, "I'm not gonna do this.

00:17:06.442 --> 00:17:09.611
It's Uncle Mike's day. I'll hold it for tomorrow."
- Yeah, "It's Uncle Mike. I'm chillin'."

00:17:09.611 --> 00:17:13.532
"Ma gave me corn yesterday."
I was like, "Relax, dude. Chill." Nah, nah.

00:17:13.532 --> 00:17:16.410
- [Laughing] Oh my gosh. Have you gotten thrown up on?

00:17:16.410 --> 00:17:17.536
- Yes.
- OK.

00:17:17.536 --> 00:17:20.122
- I've got—yeah, I've gotten throw up and I'm cool with that.
- Yeah.

00:17:20.122 --> 00:17:22.791
- Yeah, I, yeah, I deserved that one.
That was me, that was me.

00:17:22.791 --> 00:17:25.711
- [Laughing]
- But no, he's awesome and just,

00:17:25.711 --> 00:17:27.546
I never knew I could
love something so much

00:17:27.546 --> 00:17:30.174
that I never had a chance to meet.

00:17:30.174 --> 00:17:31.717
Like, literally before I even met him,

00:17:31.717 --> 00:17:33.302
it was like, oh my God, I can't wait.

00:17:33.302 --> 00:17:35.846
- Oh, I love it.
- And so I think family.

00:17:35.846 --> 00:17:38.057
- OK.
- Spending time, I think that was,

00:17:38.057 --> 00:17:41.101
that was something I
definitely learned from 2020.

00:17:41.101 --> 00:17:44.980
Um, I've always been an introvert
and kind of being good

00:17:44.980 --> 00:17:46.065
with being by myself.
- Yeah.

00:17:46.065 --> 00:17:48.067
- So I think it just reinforcing,

00:17:48.067 --> 00:17:52.237
just sitting still and getting
back to like, you know,

00:17:52.237 --> 00:17:55.240
meditating and just resetting,

00:17:55.240 --> 00:18:02.498
cuz last few years before that,
it's been a nonstop thing.

00:18:02.498 --> 00:18:04.208
And, and you know, and I
was telling myself that,

00:18:04.208 --> 00:18:05.626
yeah, after this, you know, next project,

00:18:05.626 --> 00:18:07.127
I'm gonna take a break
for a couple of years

00:18:07.127 --> 00:18:09.922
and I'm gonna just actually,
just work on, I'm gonna live.

00:18:09.922 --> 00:18:10.589
I'm gonna travel.

00:18:10.589 --> 00:18:11.673
- Have you ever taken a break?

00:18:12.883 --> 00:18:13.801
- No.

00:18:13.801 --> 00:18:14.802
- I didn't think so.

00:18:14.802 --> 00:18:16.011
- No, no, no, no.
- But it's a thing that you

00:18:16.011 --> 00:18:18.013
tell yourself to feel better.
- Yes.

00:18:18.013 --> 00:18:21.100
- [Laughing]
- You know, I wanted to live life, you know,

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:23.727
instead of just watching it,
you know what I mean?

00:18:23.727 --> 00:18:28.107
It was, and I also
wanted to experience more things

00:18:28.107 --> 00:18:32.236
so I could, you know, have more,

00:18:32.236 --> 00:18:35.114
more things to pull from in my work.

00:18:35.114 --> 00:18:37.533
- Mmm. OK, that's interesting.

00:18:37.533 --> 00:18:39.409
- You know, there's
levels to it, you know,

00:18:39.409 --> 00:18:42.246
there's—to be able to, you know,

00:18:42.246 --> 00:18:43.872
you gotta see more so you can, you know,

00:18:43.872 --> 00:18:47.084
you can talk about more and
have organic places that,

00:18:47.084 --> 00:18:48.961
of just experience, you know?

00:18:48.961 --> 00:18:51.547
Um, so that was something that, you know,

00:18:51.547 --> 00:18:54.633
obviously with everything else,
that couldn't happen as much, but it—

00:18:54.633 --> 00:18:57.511
- Yeah.
- And then, yeah, so wanting to take a break,

00:18:57.511 --> 00:18:59.429
but then I think during quarantine,

00:18:59.429 --> 00:19:05.561
my mind and appetite changed,
and it actually made me more hungry

00:19:05.561 --> 00:19:07.521
and focused and driven.

00:19:07.521 --> 00:19:12.151
So coming out of when things
started to open back up, it,

00:19:12.151 --> 00:19:16.655
I had an energy and just like
that I haven't had before.

00:19:16.655 --> 00:19:20.242
So yeah, I think way more determined.

00:19:20.242 --> 00:19:22.035
- I know you care
so much about your family.

00:19:22.035 --> 00:19:24.121
I know you care so much about your legacy.

00:19:25.122 --> 00:19:28.417
People that have money,
right, at some point,

00:19:28.417 --> 00:19:31.628
things shift from "I want to be rich"

00:19:31.628 --> 00:19:33.755
to "I want my family to be rich"

00:19:33.755 --> 00:19:38.969
to "I want my family that isn't
even alive yet to be rich."

00:19:38.969 --> 00:19:42.389
How much of a focus is
generational wealth for you?

00:19:43.849 --> 00:19:47.227
- You've been doing your homework. These are—

00:19:47.227 --> 00:19:49.146
- I'm always ready.

00:19:49.146 --> 00:19:51.690
- These are thought out. No, these are good.

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:53.442
I think about it a lot.
- Yeah.

00:19:53.442 --> 00:19:57.321
- Yeah, I think about it a lot. I think, um, you know,

00:19:57.321 --> 00:20:00.449
breaking that vicious cycle
of having to start over

00:20:00.449 --> 00:20:02.492
and over and over, knowing, you know,

00:20:02.492 --> 00:20:06.413
the family stories of
family history of, you know,

00:20:06.413 --> 00:20:10.751
what the holidays meant to
a family, of like what debt,

00:20:10.751 --> 00:20:17.925
you know, trying to meet this,
you know, making sure,

00:20:17.925 --> 00:20:20.552
you know, kids aren't left out
and this and that and,

00:20:20.552 --> 00:20:24.932
you know, doing what we can
and how that just compiles,

00:20:24.932 --> 00:20:26.141
you know, debt.

00:20:26.141 --> 00:20:27.142
And that's just one area,

00:20:27.142 --> 00:20:30.479
you know what I'm saying,
of like the system that kinda,

00:20:30.479 --> 00:20:33.273
you know, keeps us, you know,
oppressed in a lot of ways,

00:20:33.273 --> 00:20:35.859
especially financially, you know. So—
- Yeah.

00:20:36.318 --> 00:20:38.946
And in some ways by design.

00:20:38.946 --> 00:20:42.783
- Almost always by design, you know?
- Yeah.

00:20:43.367 --> 00:20:46.495
- And the education around that and lack thereof.

00:20:46.495 --> 00:20:48.538
So I know me,

00:20:48.538 --> 00:20:51.708
I'm learning things for the
first time that a lot of my

00:20:51.708 --> 00:20:55.921
family hasn't had the
opportunity to be exposed to.
- Mmhmm.

00:20:55.921 --> 00:21:00.842
- So that pressure of knowing
that and not caring about it,

00:21:00.842 --> 00:21:03.553
I think, is irresponsible.

00:21:03.553 --> 00:21:06.932
So, you know, yeah, there's
a motivation of, yeah,

00:21:06.932 --> 00:21:09.726
I want these nice things or
yeah, I wanna be comfortable,

00:21:09.726 --> 00:21:10.894
but it also comes in—it's like,

00:21:10.894 --> 00:21:14.314
yo, I can't even enjoy this comfort
if my family's not straight.

00:21:14.314 --> 00:21:17.317
Like, what do I look like
living here, feeling like this,

00:21:17.317 --> 00:21:18.235
having these things—

00:21:18.235 --> 00:21:19.903
- It would mean nothing.
Yeah.

00:21:19.903 --> 00:21:22.823
- If they don't have that
and are able to share these

00:21:22.823 --> 00:21:24.157
experiences with me?

00:21:24.157 --> 00:21:26.910
And then it trickles down to
the bigger picture of, like,

00:21:26.910 --> 00:21:30.205
you know, when I do have kids,
when I do have, you know,

00:21:30.205 --> 00:21:33.375
grandchildren and this
and that, and, you know,

00:21:33.375 --> 00:21:38.380
how do I make sure that they
don't have to burden the things

00:21:38.380 --> 00:21:41.300
that I've had to experience
growing up and starting over and

00:21:41.300 --> 00:21:42.801
having, you know, just all those things.

00:21:42.801 --> 00:21:46.722
- Obviously money is to
buy things, at its base.
- Mmhmm.

00:21:46.722 --> 00:21:49.516
- But what does having money,
like, signify for you?

00:21:49.516 --> 00:21:50.350
Does that make sense? Like,

00:21:50.350 --> 00:21:53.478
what does that mean to your life,

00:21:53.478 --> 00:21:55.981
to your family's life,
to be able to have it?

00:21:55.981 --> 00:21:57.274
- Giving us options.

00:21:57.274 --> 00:21:58.150
- Yeah. Freedom.

00:21:58.150 --> 00:21:58.984
- Freedom.

00:21:58.984 --> 00:22:00.610
- Yeah.
- I think, you know, money signifies,

00:22:00.610 --> 00:22:05.824
you know, in the basic sense
of just options and access

00:22:05.824 --> 00:22:06.742
and opportunity.

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:09.453
- You'd mentioned growing up, tell me,

00:22:09.453 --> 00:22:12.414
obviously the Invesco
QQQ Classic is in Newark.

00:22:12.414 --> 00:22:12.831
- Yep.

00:22:12.831 --> 00:22:14.624
- Tell me about your Newark.

00:22:14.624 --> 00:22:17.461
- My Newark, my Brick City.
- Yeah.

00:22:17.461 --> 00:22:19.296
- You know, that's why it was
really important to have,

00:22:19.296 --> 00:22:21.673
you know, the Legacy
Classic there, you know,

00:22:21.673 --> 00:22:24.468
at Prudential Center. Places
that I've been to growing up.

00:22:24.468 --> 00:22:26.261
Wanting to be an example, you know,

00:22:26.261 --> 00:22:29.264
for the community and the
kids there that, OK, cool,

00:22:29.264 --> 00:22:35.687
that we're bringing it
back to, you know, our roots, so to speak.

00:22:35.687 --> 00:22:39.900
I mean, my Newark is, you
know, it was fun, you know?

00:22:39.900 --> 00:22:42.944
From, you know, hooping in the
park, you know what I mean,

00:22:42.944 --> 00:22:44.946
to, you know, taking the
PATH over to the city

00:22:44.946 --> 00:22:47.657
for auditions to, you know,

00:22:47.657 --> 00:22:51.536
taking the bus home from
school and, you know,

00:22:51.536 --> 00:22:54.081
high school parties, you
know what I'm saying?

00:22:54.081 --> 00:22:57.501
Hit squad parties and
street teams, you know?
- OK!

00:22:57.501 --> 00:23:00.212
- Like, the whole, like, just the whole,
you know, what

00:23:00.212 --> 00:23:02.255
that meant was everything, you know?

00:23:02.255 --> 00:23:02.631
- Yeah.

00:23:02.631 --> 00:23:03.882
A lotta great people from Newark too.

00:23:03.882 --> 00:23:05.258
Whitney Houston, from Newark.
- Mmhmm.

00:23:05.258 --> 00:23:06.676
- I believe Shaq was born in Newark.

00:23:06.676 --> 00:23:08.095
- Shaq's born in Newark too,
yep.

00:23:08.095 --> 00:23:09.596
- Is Queen Latifah from Newark?

00:23:09.596 --> 00:23:10.972
- Queen Latifah is from Newark, yeah.

00:23:10.972 --> 00:23:14.226
- OK, yeah. So y'all produced 'em.
- We got some—
- I'm not saying it's like

00:23:14.226 --> 00:23:16.520
Gwinnett County, you know what I mean?

00:23:16.520 --> 00:23:20.065
- Yeah, where is that?
- Don't play me. Gwinnett County's in Georgia,

00:23:20.065 --> 00:23:22.651
- Georgia, OK.
- Everybody coming from out there.

00:23:22.651 --> 00:23:24.194
- OK, cool.
- We got songs about it. The North,

00:23:24.194 --> 00:23:25.987
you know, just a lil'
somethin' somethin'. It is what it is.

00:23:25.987 --> 00:23:27.781
But Newark is a great spot.

00:23:27.781 --> 00:23:30.784
I've been there a bunch,

00:23:30.784 --> 00:23:33.870
but I think that people
kind of have an idea of

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:36.915
what places are, but it's just
like this blip on the map.

00:23:36.915 --> 00:23:39.835
- Mmhmm.
- They don't, like, really know
what a place is like.

00:23:39.835 --> 00:23:41.962
Like, when I think about—
there's a basketball player,

00:23:41.962 --> 00:23:44.172
Jalen Green. He was just drafted.
- Mm.

00:23:44.172 --> 00:23:46.216
- He's from Fresno, California.
- Mmhmm.

00:23:46.216 --> 00:23:48.301
- People, like, hear California,
they're like, "Oh,

00:23:48.301 --> 00:23:49.719
this is probably some cute lil' town."

00:23:49.719 --> 00:23:54.015
He's like, "No, Fresno—"
- Yeah.
- "Fresno not playin' witchu."

00:23:54.015 --> 00:23:55.058
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
- Like, Fresno is a thing.

00:23:55.058 --> 00:23:58.145
And there are parts of Newark
that are the exact same way.

00:23:58.145 --> 00:23:58.979
- 1000 percent.

00:23:58.979 --> 00:24:00.439
- Yeah, and I don't really know if people—

00:24:00.439 --> 00:24:02.399
they're just like, "New Jersey,
it's near New York, whatever."

00:24:02.399 --> 00:24:05.277
- Yeah, I mean, it's like Newark is, you know,

00:24:05.277 --> 00:24:07.028
we got a little chip on
our shoulder, I think.

00:24:07.028 --> 00:24:09.072
- Totally.
- I think growing up—

00:24:09.072 --> 00:24:11.366
I think being next to
a city like Manhattan,

00:24:11.366 --> 00:24:15.871
New York, gives you, like,
the little brother complex

00:24:15.871 --> 00:24:16.705
a little bit.
- Mmhmm.

00:24:16.705 --> 00:24:18.790
- It's like, "Yeah, you know,
whatchu talkin' about?"

00:24:18.790 --> 00:24:19.624
Like, "Nah, we just as good.

00:24:19.624 --> 00:24:21.209
We ain't got no sales tax on clothes.

00:24:21.209 --> 00:24:22.836
Y'all be coming over here
shopping all the time."

00:24:22.836 --> 00:24:25.672
Like, it's like we're very
proud of the things that we,

00:24:25.672 --> 00:24:29.384
that we have and is known
for, but it makes us scrappy,

00:24:29.384 --> 00:24:31.219
and it makes us, like, determined.
- Totally.

00:24:31.219 --> 00:24:35.265
- I think going to, you know,
West New York or Weehawken

00:24:35.265 --> 00:24:37.476
and looking over at the
skyline and seeing the city,

00:24:37.476 --> 00:24:39.394
seeing those big buildings
and what that represents

00:24:39.394 --> 00:24:40.812
of like, "Oh, I gotta get there one day.

00:24:40.812 --> 00:24:43.148
I gotta–you know, I
want a piece of that."

00:24:43.148 --> 00:24:43.940
You know?
- Yeah.

00:24:43.940 --> 00:24:48.612
- I think it gives you a
motivation and a drive a bit.

00:24:48.612 --> 00:24:56.953
But Newark is, you know,
it's tough. You know, it's,

00:24:56.953 --> 00:25:00.957
you know, it was hard
for sure, but, you know,

00:25:00.957 --> 00:25:03.418
it's got a lotta promise and
a lotta talent, you know,

00:25:03.418 --> 00:25:07.005
and a lotta opportunity there as well.

00:25:07.005 --> 00:25:10.634
You know, it just—

00:25:12.427 --> 00:25:16.723
just the timing, you know? I think,
you know, it needs people to reinvest,

00:25:16.723 --> 00:25:19.518
you know, back in. You know,
pour back into the city.

00:25:20.936 --> 00:25:24.731
And I think there's places
like the Fresnos and, you know,

00:25:24.731 --> 00:25:26.358
and the Newarks and, you know,

00:25:26.358 --> 00:25:28.318
all these little places
that are, you know,

00:25:28.318 --> 00:25:29.861
throughout the country that, you know,

00:25:29.861 --> 00:25:32.572
produce talent and people that, you know,

00:25:32.572 --> 00:25:36.493
kinda somehow make it out
of the circumstances in their

00:25:36.493 --> 00:25:39.538
environments in a big way that, you know,

00:25:39.538 --> 00:25:41.331
turn it back around
and be like, "All right,

00:25:41.331 --> 00:25:47.546
how do I help reinvest
and help nurture the place

00:25:47.546 --> 00:25:48.421
that I came from?"

00:25:48.421 --> 00:25:51.049
You know, so—
- So—
- It's a process.

00:25:51.049 --> 00:25:52.551
- I have to say, I hear you
talking about, you know,

00:25:52.551 --> 00:25:54.177
reinvesting, pouring back in.
- Mmhmm.

00:25:54.177 --> 00:25:56.721
- I think about this Classic,
giving back to the community

00:25:56.721 --> 00:25:59.224
and, you know, this deep
love of Black people,

00:25:59.224 --> 00:26:02.394
I can't help but think about Killmonger.
- Mmhmm.

00:26:02.394 --> 00:26:05.063
- And after "Black Panther" came out,

00:26:05.063 --> 00:26:07.399
I swear I had this debate
for, like, three months.

00:26:07.399 --> 00:26:10.986
Obviously there's no definitive
answer. Opinion is opinion.

00:26:12.153 --> 00:26:14.364
But is Killmonger a villain?

00:26:14.364 --> 00:26:15.824
- No.

00:26:15.824 --> 00:26:17.325
- Please tell me why. I agree.

00:26:17.325 --> 00:26:20.453
I think, like, he's an antagonist.
- Yeah, exactly.

00:26:20.453 --> 00:26:25.375
- But I don't know if
he's, like, a bad person.

00:26:25.375 --> 00:26:26.710
What is your take on it?

00:26:27.544 --> 00:26:30.005
- My take on Erik— - And I'm also so right.

00:26:30.005 --> 00:26:32.716
I'm so happy that I can
say we agree on this.

00:26:33.883 --> 00:26:35.927
No one agreed with me, OK?
- Yeah.

00:26:35.927 --> 00:26:36.970
- Please continue.

00:26:38.054 --> 00:26:39.514
You know that feeling when you're like—

00:26:39.514 --> 00:26:41.474
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- "I'm one up on you." Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:26:41.474 --> 00:26:46.229
How you gonna argue with Killmonger?!
All right, tell me—

00:26:46.229 --> 00:26:48.481
- That's a good one. That's a good one.
- Gimme your take.

00:26:48.481 --> 00:26:52.819
- No, I think, I think he was a necessary
part of the conversation.

00:26:52.819 --> 00:26:56.197
- Yes.
- You know, I think, you know,

00:26:56.197 --> 00:26:59.576
he cared about his people just as much as,

00:26:59.576 --> 00:27:01.119
you know, T'Challa.

00:27:01.119 --> 00:27:06.041
He just had a different way of
going about getting it done.

00:27:06.041 --> 00:27:10.670
- Yeah.
- I think he was a historian that studied

00:27:10.670 --> 00:27:15.425
history, you know? The
history of, you know,

00:27:15.425 --> 00:27:18.011
government and oppression.

00:27:18.011 --> 00:27:20.930
And, you know, Erik's a really smart guy,

00:27:20.930 --> 00:27:21.765
you know what I'm saying?

00:27:21.765 --> 00:27:25.143
MIT graduate. The guy is
like—he's very intelligent.

00:27:25.143 --> 00:27:30.273
And he saw that there
was only really one way

00:27:30.273 --> 00:27:32.400
to change things, you know?

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:37.489
So he went about that, and I
don't think his argument was

00:27:37.489 --> 00:27:38.615
completely wrong.

00:27:38.615 --> 00:27:41.034
I don't think T'Challa's
argument was completely wrong.

00:27:41.034 --> 00:27:43.244
I think it was a necessary conversation

00:27:43.244 --> 00:27:47.832
that needed to be had. But, you know,

00:27:47.832 --> 00:27:48.750
it's a movie also.

00:27:48.750 --> 00:27:50.335
So you gotta, you know—

00:27:51.211 --> 00:27:52.921
- And that conversation makes it good, right?

00:27:52.921 --> 00:27:54.631
- I'm willing to take life.
You know what I'm saying?

00:27:54.631 --> 00:27:56.466
To do whatever it takes, you know,

00:27:56.466 --> 00:27:58.551
and this is what I've been taught.

00:27:58.551 --> 00:28:01.596
This is what I've been
shown that works, you know?

00:28:01.596 --> 00:28:03.431
So you can not like it,

00:28:03.431 --> 00:28:08.019
but there's this other version
of trying to get change done.

00:28:08.019 --> 00:28:09.896
And it's kind of taken a
little too long for me.

00:28:09.896 --> 00:28:11.856
I ain't got that time, you know?

00:28:11.856 --> 00:28:15.360
And I, yeah, I guess, but
I'm doing it like this.

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:16.695
- Yeah. I love it.

00:28:16.695 --> 00:28:18.029
And I totally agree.

00:28:18.029 --> 00:28:21.741
It's like wanting of the same
thing with different methods.

00:28:21.741 --> 00:28:24.536
I mean, maybe a little
difference in the, in the thing,

00:28:24.536 --> 00:28:26.788
but the result is pretty similar, right?

00:28:26.788 --> 00:28:28.790
Just the empowerment of Black people.

00:28:28.790 --> 00:28:31.167
They both wanted that.
It's like, you know,

00:28:31.167 --> 00:28:33.420
people always kind of do
the whole Martin and Malcolm

00:28:33.420 --> 00:28:34.462
approach.
- Yup.

00:28:34.462 --> 00:28:36.172
- They wanted the same thing. They just had,

00:28:36.172 --> 00:28:38.299
they just kind of had some
different ways of going about it.

00:28:38.299 --> 00:28:39.926
But I'm just, right now, I'm just happy.

00:28:39.926 --> 00:28:41.886
I can't even really
focus on what you said.

00:28:41.886 --> 00:28:43.138
- It's all good. You got that one.
You got that one.

00:28:43.138 --> 00:28:44.139
- Yeah, this is a win for me.

00:28:44.139 --> 00:28:46.307
And that's truly all I care about.

00:28:47.475 --> 00:28:49.144
So I know obviously you love sports.

00:28:49.144 --> 00:28:51.146
We got to talk about some sports.

00:28:51.146 --> 00:28:55.400
I have something for you.
I had a friend of mine

00:28:55.400 --> 00:28:58.486
in the Midwest, has a
little something that

00:28:58.486 --> 00:29:00.155
they want to say to you.

00:29:00.155 --> 00:29:02.407
So we're gonna, we're
gonna play that for you.

00:29:02.407 --> 00:29:05.744
- Michael B. Jordan, Tom Izzo here.
- Oh, my man. OK. Izzo.

00:29:05.744 --> 00:29:10.999
- I want to thank you so much for being a college basketball fan
and even more for being a Michigan State fan.
- Oh, that’s dope.

00:29:10.999 --> 00:29:12.500
- We gotta get you some gears

00:29:12.500 --> 00:29:18.798
so the next time you’re on TV, supporting the
Green and White, we’ll get you and Magic together or something. 
- Oh, that's dope.

00:29:18.798 --> 00:29:21.092
- But I also wanted
to tell you that we'd like

00:29:21.092 --> 00:29:24.304
to invite you back to
Midnight Madness or to one of

00:29:24.304 --> 00:29:27.223
those big games in our arena,
and the place would go crazy.

00:29:27.223 --> 00:29:30.435
- That's nuts. You know, I've never been.
- So once again, Michael, thank you for everything you’ve done.

00:29:30.435 --> 00:29:31.269
- You gotta go.

00:29:31.269 --> 00:29:33.855
- Congratulations for
all the success you've had.

00:29:33.855 --> 00:29:36.274
Keep your hand in college basketball.

00:29:36.274 --> 00:29:38.693
- There it is. That's
awesome. That's great.

00:29:38.693 --> 00:29:39.778
[Michael celebrating]

00:29:39.778 --> 00:29:42.572
- You have to go. Yeah,
I got you. I got you.

00:29:42.572 --> 00:29:44.491
You've gotta go now. Go to Midnight Madness.

00:29:44.491 --> 00:29:46.576
Be the guest. Go to a big game.

00:29:46.576 --> 00:29:48.119
- That's cool. That’s cool. Yeah, man.

00:29:48.119 --> 00:29:49.496
- He's the best. Shoutout to him.

00:29:49.496 --> 00:29:52.415
Shoutout to Mike.
Shoutout to Max. Everybody.
- Appreciate it. OK. Wooh.

00:29:52.415 --> 00:29:53.333
- Yeah, I got you.

00:29:53.333 --> 00:29:54.334
I'll make sure I send that to you

00:29:54.334 --> 00:29:55.668
so you have, you have it.

00:29:55.668 --> 00:29:56.961
- So this what happens when you do your podcast?

00:29:56.961 --> 00:30:02.175
- I always have a little somethin’. You know,
shoutout to Bobby cuz me and Bobby were talking about it.

00:30:02.175 --> 00:30:03.718
I was like, I know he
likes Michigan State.

00:30:03.718 --> 00:30:05.220
We were debating the Giants. I was like,

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:07.305
I'm pretty sure he's a Giants fan.

00:30:07.305 --> 00:30:11.059
I was like, but I think he's
more of a Michigan State fan.

00:30:11.059 --> 00:30:12.977
So, shoutout to Bobby,
who's in here somewhere

00:30:12.977 --> 00:30:16.856
acting like he
don't hear me. But, yeah.

00:30:16.856 --> 00:30:18.316
So I'll tell him that you saw that.

00:30:18.316 --> 00:30:19.859
Why Michigan State?

00:30:19.859 --> 00:30:22.362
- Growing up as a kid just
always watching them on TV.

00:30:22.362 --> 00:30:24.948
Like, it wasn't really like
a rhyme or reason. Then,

00:30:24.948 --> 00:30:27.325
you know, realizing, you
know, Magic Johnson came out

00:30:27.325 --> 00:30:30.119
of there, you know? And then
just watching those NCAA games with,

00:30:30.119 --> 00:30:32.247
like, Mateen Cleaves playing for them.

00:30:32.247 --> 00:30:34.749
And then, you know, ultimately
watching Draymond play

00:30:34.749 --> 00:30:38.336
through there. So it was
just for me, you know,

00:30:38.336 --> 00:30:43.007
I just always, you know,
dug the organization.
- Yeah.

00:30:43.007 --> 00:30:45.426
- There wasn't really like—it's not no real deep.

00:30:45.426 --> 00:30:46.511
It's just—

00:30:46.511 --> 00:30:47.136
- Well, you got lucky.

00:30:47.136 --> 00:30:47.720
It's a good pick.

00:30:47.720 --> 00:30:51.140
Like, that's a good school
to just kind of fall into,

00:30:51.140 --> 00:30:54.143
into being a fan of. You at this point,

00:30:54.143 --> 00:30:57.021
you are kind of like a
figure in sports culture too,

00:30:57.021 --> 00:30:57.480
you know?

00:30:57.480 --> 00:31:00.817
Like, you sit courtside at the
games. And I want to ask you

00:31:00.817 --> 00:31:02.944
about the process of sitting
courtside because to you,

00:31:02.944 --> 00:31:04.571
it probably feels very normal,

00:31:04.571 --> 00:31:06.948
but the majority of humans
have never done that.

00:31:06.948 --> 00:31:08.908
They've never sat courtside.

00:31:08.908 --> 00:31:09.492
They, No. 1,

00:31:09.492 --> 00:31:11.160
don't know that once you
sit courtside, you

00:31:11.160 --> 00:31:11.995
can't go back.

00:31:14.289 --> 00:31:17.083
- It's kind of like first class a little bit.
- I was just about to say.

00:31:17.083 --> 00:31:18.084
- Like, oh, this is nice.

00:31:18.084 --> 00:31:19.878
- It's like, I'll catch y'all later.

00:31:19.878 --> 00:31:22.881
Cuz I ain't going back.
- Fresh cookies. What? Free drinks. What?

00:31:22.881 --> 00:31:24.799
- Get the blanket and the
pillow and Delta One.

00:31:24.799 --> 00:31:28.636
- You ain't walking past first class no more.
It's tough.

00:31:28.636 --> 00:31:31.973
- So why is it? Why is sitting
courtside just, like,

00:31:31.973 --> 00:31:34.976
unlike anything. Like, tell
someone who's never been

00:31:34.976 --> 00:31:36.144
what it is like.

00:31:37.145 --> 00:31:38.855
- I'm coming from a hooper's perspective.

00:31:38.855 --> 00:31:42.025
So it's like playing in, like,
tournaments and games and like,

00:31:42.025 --> 00:31:45.570
you know, competing, you
know, in organized sports.

00:31:45.570 --> 00:31:47.864
When you're sitting courtside,

00:31:47.864 --> 00:31:51.034
it's almost like being back at the gym.

00:31:51.034 --> 00:31:52.243
You know what I'm saying?
You're hearing the sneakers.

00:31:52.243 --> 00:31:53.828
You're hearing the talking,
you know what I'm saying?

00:31:53.828 --> 00:31:54.412
You're hearing,

00:31:54.412 --> 00:31:58.333
you're paying attention to
the game within the game.
- Yes. Which is the better part.

00:31:58.333 --> 00:31:59.042
- That's the part—

00:31:59.042 --> 00:32:01.085
listen, I don't even know
if endorsements would

00:32:01.085 --> 00:32:03.129
be able to handle, like, paying for audio.

00:32:03.129 --> 00:32:07.091
Like, if I can pay for, like,
audio of guys, like, everybody,

00:32:07.091 --> 00:32:09.177
everybody loses their brand deals.
Brand deals out of here.

00:32:09.177 --> 00:32:10.845
Everything is done actually.

00:32:10.845 --> 00:32:16.768
But that part of the game
to experience is—you can't,

00:32:16.768 --> 00:32:18.853
it's really hard to explain
to somebody who hasn't been

00:32:18.853 --> 00:32:19.896
there, but that's

00:32:19.896 --> 00:32:23.232
I think the specialness of
sitting courtside and being—

00:32:23.232 --> 00:32:25.360
- Best thing you've heard courtside?

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:26.527
- Oh, I can't say that.

00:32:26.527 --> 00:32:28.905
- Oh, it’s a secret?
- No, no.

00:32:28.905 --> 00:32:31.115
I mean, it's like out of respect
for like what's being said.

00:32:31.115 --> 00:32:31.908
It's, like, really tough.

00:32:31.908 --> 00:32:35.703
I think, like, uh, I'm trying to think what, uh...

00:32:36.746 --> 00:32:43.252
you know, just having
moments where like, you know,

00:32:43.252 --> 00:32:47.924
let's say, like, LeBron or
whatever, and he'll come down.

00:32:47.924 --> 00:32:49.926
Not that he tells you
exactly what he's gonna do,

00:32:49.926 --> 00:32:51.427
but he insinuates he's about to do it.

00:32:51.427 --> 00:32:54.305
And then actually like doing
it and then kind of giving you

00:32:54.305 --> 00:32:55.890
a nod like, “I told you so.”

00:32:55.890 --> 00:32:56.849
It's like one of those times.

00:32:56.849 --> 00:32:57.725
- OK, tell me one of
those moments, like, that

00:32:57.725 --> 00:32:59.727
we all saw that you're like he said,

00:32:59.727 --> 00:33:02.313
he was about to do this, and he went and did it.

00:33:02.313 --> 00:33:05.149
- It was more than on the
defensive end. And it was,

00:33:05.149 --> 00:33:07.235
I can't remember the team that
he was playing, but it was,

00:33:07.235 --> 00:33:11.197
it was—somebody kept

00:33:12.240 --> 00:33:15.118
getting behind, getting
behind the defense or whatever

00:33:15.118 --> 00:33:16.452
and had easy layups.

00:33:16.452 --> 00:33:17.036
You know what I'm saying?

00:33:17.036 --> 00:33:18.746
Somebody wasn't getting
back or something like that.

00:33:18.746 --> 00:33:22.667
And I remember him making an
adjustment and actually being

00:33:22.667 --> 00:33:24.752
last man back the last
time—I want so say

00:33:24.752 --> 00:33:26.629
it was outlet, fast break,
something like that.

00:33:26.629 --> 00:33:27.797
And he ended up like, you know,

00:33:27.797 --> 00:33:29.882
breaking up a pass or whatever
the case may be. And then,

00:33:29.882 --> 00:33:30.925
you know what I'm saying, he got amped up.

00:33:30.925 --> 00:33:32.677
Everybody came over and
dapped him up or whatever.

00:33:32.677 --> 00:33:35.138
And it was a little like, yeah,
like, I made the adjustment.

00:33:35.138 --> 00:33:39.559
Like, his IQ and his court awareness,

00:33:39.559 --> 00:33:42.603
you know what I'm saying? And
his basketball IQ is so high.

00:33:42.603 --> 00:33:45.815
Like, just knowing what needs
to be done in the adjustments

00:33:45.815 --> 00:33:48.317
to be made, I think was really, really,

00:33:48.317 --> 00:33:50.111
it was really cool to see
up close and personal.

00:33:50.111 --> 00:33:53.114
So that's just a, can't
remember exact moment,

00:33:53.114 --> 00:33:55.533
but that's something that I
do remember, you know, him—

00:33:55.533 --> 00:33:57.035
- I know totally what you mean. It was,

00:33:58.244 --> 00:34:01.748
that was the best part for
me about being in the bubble

00:34:01.748 --> 00:34:03.458
cuz you're essentially
courtside every game and

00:34:03.458 --> 00:34:04.292
there's no people in there.

00:34:04.292 --> 00:34:07.837
So when I say you hear like
the smallest little thing,

00:34:08.337 --> 00:34:10.757
it was just great. I had an,

00:34:10.757 --> 00:34:12.508
I don't want to say like a
new respect for the game,

00:34:12.508 --> 00:34:14.343
cuz I really respect the game,

